    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0598" n="559" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v13/pg559"/>

                <head>139. Memorandum From John Renner and Michel Oksenberg of the National Security
                    Council Staff to the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Brzezinski)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d139-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1978-10-06">October 6, 1978</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item> PRC on US–China Trade</item>
                </list>
                <p>As you know from evening reports, we have been exploring the feasibility of
                    expanding trade with China and negotiating a US–China trade agreement.
                    Preliminary analysis suggests that there are no unsurmountable technical
                    obstacles. Thus, it is worthwhile to examine whether it is in our national
                    interest to move in this direction and to assess the probable strategic
                    consequences.</p>
                <p>To begin preparing for a PRC review of this question, we have written the outline
                    at Tab A to serve as the basis for drafting the paper <pb facs="0599" n="560"
                        xml:id="pg560"/> to be considered at the PRC meeting.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d139-f2"/> We plan to call together a small group from interested
                    agencies and assign drafting responsibilities.</p>
                <p>It is not our intention to reopen PRM 24, Part 3, on dual purpose items.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d139-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Negotiating a trade agreement with China would enable us to put the question of
                    orderly marketing in a larger context. In fact, the Trade Act of 1974 requires
                    it.</p>
                <p>While settlement of the financial claims issue is not a prerequisite for a trade
                    agreement, the Johnson Debt Default Act makes it illegal for any American
                    institution or person to make a loan to any foreign government in default of its
                    financial obligations to the United States. As a practical matter, we should try
                    to move the trade and claims negotiations along on parallel tracks.</p>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">Recommendation</p>
                <p>That you approve this approach.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d139-f4"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
